00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.670 align:start position:0%
european ministers responsible for  employment and social affairs meeting in  brussels will discuss new rules to  better protect workers who are  temporarily posted abroad by their  employers the idea is to ensure certain  minimum terms and conditions of  employment on issues like working hours  minimum wage health and safety  provisions and equal opportunities as  well as to halt social dumping  the council will also take stock of the  progress on a directive that aims to  break the glass ceiling that continues  to bar female talent from top positions  in europe's biggest companies  the new rules would oblige companies  listed on stock exchanges in the eu to  bring in transparent recruitment  procedures  so that by twenty twenty at least forty  percent of their non-executive directors  are women  ministers will also take stock of how  the youth guarantee and the youth  employment initiatives are being  implemented in the member states these  aim to give young people a better chance  to enter the labour market  health and consumer affairs ministers  will exchange views on draft new rules  on medical devices such as x-ray  machines heart valves or breast implants  with the idea to better protect patients  and dissuade fraud  ministers will also review the state of  play in the negotiations on future eu  tobacco rules which aim at making  tobacco products less attractive the  plan is to come to an agreement before  the end of the year but the split over  how to regulate e-cigarettes is not yet  over
 

